# FDT Android — native UI milestone

Native Kotlin / Jetpack Compose UI, not a WebView. This is explicitly a DEMO, not the requested finished packet capture engine. No real PICOOC traffic is captured or analyzed.

## Implemented
- Dark Material 3 UI with Overview, Traffic, Findings and Session tabs.
- Start/stop synthetic event generation, bounded 300-entry display buffers and 60-sample sent/received chart.
- Separate synthetic packet and HTTP views, host search, cleartext filter and pause-view control independent of simulation.
- Fixture finding details with hidden sample values and session deletion confirmation.
- ViewModel keeps session state through activity recreation. No persistence or network permissions.

## Build
Open this android directory in Android Studio with JDK 17 and Android SDK 36. Toolchain pins: AGP 8.13.2, Kotlin 2.2.21, Gradle 8.13. This source has NOT been compiled or device-tested by the authoring assistant; no APK is included.

The Gradle wrapper binaries/scripts are not included in this initial source drop. With Gradle 8.13 installed, run `gradle wrapper --gradle-version 8.13`, then `./gradlew assembleDebug`. Commit the generated wrapper files after verification. On Windows use gradlew.bat. Expected APK after successful build: app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk.

## Architecture
Compose UI -> InspectorViewModel -> StateFlow<Session>. The current producer is synthetic and should be replaced behind a traffic-source abstraction before real capture integration. Fixtures are NOT a health detector. Charts show generated bytes per second, not measured device bandwidth.

## Remaining work before PICOOC testing
1. Select and license-review a packet forwarding/capture engine; do not create a TUN black hole.
2. Implement consent, foreground service, app scoping, stop/revoke recovery, packet drops and lifecycle handling.
3. Normalize real packets and reconstruct supported HTTP streams under strict memory limits.
4. Implement and test health-field detection with provenance, partial-message states and evidence.
5. Validate with an authorized real PICOOC synchronization session. HTTPS visibility must not imply cleartext exposure.
6. Add automated tests, CI, accessibility checks and emulator/device validation.

Bluetooth scale traffic and TLS interception are outside the first network-capture milestone. Preserve the existing root web frontend during migration. Never commit real health captures, tokens or credentials.

